WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.860
On April 15th 2019, firefighters charged into the

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.560
Notre Dame cathedral to quell a blazing fire.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.520
They took careful precautions while

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.300
spraying down the 850-year-old Parisian monument,

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.580
like using lower water pressure,

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:18.080
and avoiding stained glass vulnerable to shattering.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.480
They also followed a plan to save priceless relics and

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.204
works of art.

00:00:23.204 --> 00:00:25.900
In the days, following firefighters gave way to

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.520
a new team on the front line:

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.167
restoration scientists from the Laboratory for Historical Monuments.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.700
These researchers were tasked with evaluating the

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.580
condition of the damaged, stone, mortar and metal, and

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:41.960
guiding engineers on the best way to restore the cathedral.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.980
Even as they try to reclaim what was lost,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.220
the lab researchers and others are also

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.160
analyzing samples of wood and stone for

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.020
clues to the cathedral's medieval past.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.940
Their first order of business: stabilizing the cathedral.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.160
The lead roof and the wooden timbers that supported it

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.410
were mostly gone, and there were holes in

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.580
the vaulted stone ceiling.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.620
That threatened a delicate balance of forces

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.040
between the vaults, which push outward on the cathedral walls,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.219
and the flying buttresses which provide an inward stabilizing force.

00:01:14.219 --> 00:01:16.940
In order to steady the structure, temporary wood

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.320
supports were installed to prevent the buttresses from

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.480
toppling the walls.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.869
To restore the balance of forces, researchers also need

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:27.040
to understand how the stone in the walls and vault

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.780
were altered by the fire.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.380
Most of Notre Dame was constructed with limestone--

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.840
an iconic stone used all over Paris,

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.580
including at the Louvre.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:39.479
At 800°C, limestone can turn to a

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:41.780
soft white powder known as lime.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.400
Even before this point, at 300°C,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.380
fire can begin to reduce its strength.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.960
One way to estimate these changes is by

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.860
looking at the color of the stone.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.120
Beige indicates a sturdy structure, while a red or black

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.860
surface means the fire has damaged its internal structure.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.560
The high porosity of limestone presents another complication.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.440
As firefighters hosed down the Cathedral, some stones

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.610
gained almost a third of their weight in water--

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.940
further changing the balance of forces between the vaults and wall.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.560
The laboratory researchers are also grappling with what

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.320
happened to the lead roof.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.440
Parts of it reached the melting point of 300°C,

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.440
dripping into the vaults and gutters,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.900
but some of the lead became airborne.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.460
Yellow smoke billowed from the Cathedral as a result.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.360
The lead powder that settled after the fire

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.560
is toxic to humans, making researchers take careful

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.920
precautions when working in the cathedral.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.370
Before restoration even begins, much of the

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.560
cathedral must be decontaminated, and the mangled

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.420
scaffolding put in place for renovations prior to the fire

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.500
must be removed.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.680
Meanwhile, the exposed interior and fallen fragments of

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.340
the cathedral have provided new opportunities for research.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.020
One researcher is using stone samples collected from

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:03.620
the cathedral to learn about the quarries they came from.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.600
Other materials, like the wood from the attic beams,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.580
tell the climatic history of the 12th and 13th century.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.820
The long and narrow shape of the beams also indicate

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.570
that these 100-year-old trees were

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.440
grown alongside many other trees--

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.000
and maybe were even farmed with the intention of using

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.280
the wood to build the Cathedral.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.880
These finds provide glimpses into the detailed

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.480
construction of Notre Dame.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.815
It took a 182 years to complete, with signature pieces

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.880
like the spire added well after in the 19th century.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.669
Centuries from now, this fire will be just another chapter

00:03:37.669 --> 00:03:39.540
in the story of Notre Dame.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.460
But the collaborative research to understand its past,

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.520
and the careful plans to reconstruct the cathedral,

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.959
will allow future generations to continue to

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.500
be inspired by the iconic structure.

